#- Tapes
#-
#- Every value in Aurora is a tape: a fixed run of bytes, big-endian and
#- right-aligned, 8 bytes by default. The bracket form is a literal that fills
#- one byte per value.
#-
#- A tape behaves as a shift register. "pull" shifts it left and lets the value
#- in at the right end; "push" shifts it right and lets the value in at the
#- left. Whatever reaches the far end is discarded: the width never changes.
#-
#- Run: aurora run examples/tapes.ar

ident t = [1, 2, 3];
printb t;

#- An empty literal is a tape of zeros, the same value as 0.
printb [];

#- pull: 4 enters at the right.
printb pull t 4;

#- push: 5 enters at the left, and the far byte falls off.
printb push t 5;

#- head keeps the first n significant bytes, tail drops them.
ident five = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
printb head five 2;
printb tail five 2;

#- The index is taken modulo the tape width, so it can never be out of bounds.
ident full = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8];
printb tail full 18;

#- Everything is a tape, so the operations work on any value, not just literals.
ident n = 1;
printb pull n 2;

#- The result is an ordinary value, usable anywhere.
ident built = pull [1, 2] 3;
printb built + 1;
